-- title:
--   Time evolution $\Phi_t=e^{-it[H,\cdot]+t\mathcal L}$
-- statement:
--   The **time evolution** for time $t$ generated by $\dot\rho=-i[H,\rho]+\mathcal L(\rho)$ is the superoperator exponential
--
--   $$\Phi_t=\exp\bigl(-it[H,\cdot\,]+t\mathcal L\bigr),\qquad \rho(t)=\Phi_t[\rho(0)].$$
--
--   This is the map $\Phi_{1/T,t}$ of Theorem 1.1 and $e^{-it[H,\cdot]+\mathcal Lt}$ of eq. (48).
--
--   **Formalization Note** The generator is written as a matrix on vectorized operators and exponentiated with the matrix exponential; since the generator is linear this is exactly $e^{t\hat{\mathcal L}}$.
-- source:
--   Balasubramanian, Davydova, Lin, A passive self-correcting quantum memory in three dimensions, arXiv:2605.10943, eq. (45), p. 19; eq. (48), p. 21; Theorem 1.1, p. 4

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_SelfCorrect3D_Memory_generator
import Definitions.Def_SelfCorrect3D_Memory_superMatrix
open Matrix

namespace SelfCorrect3D.Memory

/-- **Time evolution** `Φ_t = exp(−it[H, ·] + t𝓛)` for time `t`. Balasubramanian, Davydova, Lin, *A passive self-correcting quantum memory in three dimensions*, arXiv:2605.10943,
eq. (45), p. 19 ("The solution to this equation of motion is written as
ρ(t) = exp(−it[H, ·] + t𝓛)[ρ(0)]"), eq. (48), p. 21, and `Φ_{1/T,t}` of Theorem 1.1, p. 4.

**Formalization Note.** The superoperator `𝓛̂ = −i[H, ·] + 𝓛` is written as a matrix on
vectorized operators (`superMatrix`), exponentiated with `NormedSpace.exp` (matrix exponential),
applied to `vec ρ` and un-vectorized. This is exactly `e^{t𝓛̂} ρ` because `𝓛̂` is linear. -/
noncomputable def evolution {n m : ℕ} (H : Op n) (J : Fin m → Op n) (t : ℝ) (ρ : Op n) : Op n :=
  Matrix.of fun x y =>
    (NormedSpace.exp ((t : ℂ) • superMatrix (generator H J)) *ᵥ fun q => ρ q.1 q.2) (x, y)

end SelfCorrect3D.Memory


